      {
        "source_handle": "P0297",
        "title": "The gut microbiome in lung cancer: from pathogenesis to precision therapy",
        "year": "2025",
        "doi": "10.3389/fmicb.2025.1606684",
        "paper_id": "8211fc48bb00f738157c8859ad0748cc7d220f09",
        "material_depth": "fulltext",
        "material_status": "resolved",
        "aliases": [],
        "study_summary_A": {
          "approach": "The authors conducted a narrative synthesis of literature from microbiology, immunology, and oncology. They organized the content into thematic sections: introduction to the gut-lung axis, alterations in microbiota composition and metabolism in lung cancer patients, microbial biomarkers for early detection, prediction of immunotherapy response, modulation of drug efficacy, microbiota-targeted therapeutic interventions, and mechanistic insights. The review integrates findings from clinical studies, animal models, and multi-omics analyses to construct a cohesive understanding of the gut-lung axis in lung cancer.",
          "contribution_and_limits": [
            {
              "contribution": "Provides a comprehensive overview of the gut-lung axis in lung cancer, linking specific microbial taxa and metabolites to clinical outcomes in diagnosis and immunotherapy.",
              "limits": "As a narrative review, it does not present new primary data or perform a systematic meta-analysis. The causal relationships described are largely inferred from correlative clinical studies and preclinical models, which may vary across populations."
            },
            {
              "contribution": "Identifies specific microbial signatures (e.g., A. muciniphila, SCFA producers) and metabolic profiles as potential biomarkers for predicting ICI response.",
              "limits": "The generalizability of these biomarkers depends on the heterogeneity of patient cohorts and sequencing methodologies used in the cited studies, which are not standardized in this review."
            },
            {
              "contribution": "Discusses therapeutic interventions like FMT and probiotics as adjuncts to cancer therapy, highlighting their potential to overcome resistance.",
              "limits": "Clinical translation faces challenges including interindividual variability, lack of standardized protocols for FMT/probiotics, and long-term safety concerns, which are noted but not resolved."
            }
          ],
          "key_findings": [
            {
              "conditions": "In lung cancer patients compared to healthy controls",
              "finding": "Patients exhibit dysbiosis characterized by increased abundance of Ruminococcus, Enterobacteriaceae, Lachnospiraceae, and decreased levels of Faecalibacterium, Bifidobacterium, and Veillonella. Metabolic profiles show reduced SCFA production and increased lactate."
            },
            {
              "conditions": "For early detection of lung cancer",
              "finding": "A panel of 13 microbial biomarkers was identified with high diagnostic potential, achieving an AUC of 92.4% in discovery and 67.7% in validation cohorts using a Patient Discrimination Index (PDI)."
            },
            {
              "conditions": "Predicting response to Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors (ICIs)",
              "finding": "Baseline presence of Akkermansia muciniphila, Faecalibacterium spp., and Clostridiales correlates with improved ICI outcomes. Higher levels of acetate, propionate, and butyrate are associated with prolonged progression-free survival. A TOPOSCORE integrating species interaction groups and A. muciniphila abundance predicts individual responses."
            },
            {
              "conditions": "Impact of antibiotics and probiotics on ICI efficacy",
              "finding": "Antibiotic use prior to ICI therapy is associated with reduced overall survival and impaired efficacy. Probiotic supplementation (e.g., Bifidobacterium, Clostridium butyricum) may enhance ICI efficacy and improve survival outcomes in NSCLC patients."
            },
            {
              "conditions": "Mechanisms of action",
              "finding": "Gut microbes modulate lung immunity via metabolites (SCFAs), antigen presentation, and immune cell trafficking. Specific bacteria like A. muciniphila can translocate to tumor tissues, restructure the intratumoral ecosystem, and restore exhausted CD8+ T cells, enhancing anti-PD-1 therapy efficacy."
            }
          ],
          "paper_kind": "review",
          "problem_or_question": "To synthesize existing evidence regarding how the gut microbiome influences lung cancer pathogenesis and treatment efficacy, particularly in the context of immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs), and to identify potential therapeutic strategies and biomarkers derived from this interaction.",
          "research_scope": "The review focuses on the gut-lung axis in the context of lung cancer, specifically examining non-small cell lung carcinoma (NSCLC) and small cell lung carcinoma. It covers pathogenesis, early detection biomarkers, mechanisms of immune modulation, and therapeutic interventions including immunotherapy response prediction and microbiota-targeted treatments.",
          "work_summary": "This is a narrative review synthesizing current knowledge on the role of the gut microbiome in lung cancer development, diagnosis, and treatment. It outlines the bidirectional communication between intestinal microbiota and pulmonary health via immune, neural, and metabolic pathways. The paper highlights microbial signatures predictive of disease and therapy response, discusses microbiota-targeted interventions such as probiotics and fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), and elucidates mechanistic insights into microbial-immune crosstalk. It aims to provide a comprehensive framework for leveraging the gut microbiome in personalized lung cancer management."
        },
        "review_planning_B": {
          "planning_summary": "This review serves as a foundational resource for understanding the gut-lung axis in lung cancer, particularly its impact on immunotherapy efficacy. It provides detailed descriptions of microbial signatures associated with ICI response, mechanistic pathways involving metabolites and immune cells, and emerging therapeutic strategies. It is highly relevant for facets concerning how the microbiome modulates ICI efficacy and the underlying mechanisms.",
          "facet_contributions": [
            {
              "boundaries": "Focuses primarily on lung cancer; while it mentions other cancers briefly, the detailed evidence is lung-specific. Causal inference is limited to observational associations and preclinical models.",
              "contribution": "Details the correlation between specific gut microbiota compositions (e.g., A. muciniphila, SCFA-producing bacteria) and improved clinical outcomes in solid tumors treated with ICIs. It also notes that antibiotic use negatively impacts ICI efficacy.",
              "facet_id": "F1",
              "possible_uses": "Can be used to support arguments about the positive or negative influence of specific microbial taxa on ICI efficacy in solid tumors. Provides examples of predictive biomarkers."
            },
            {
              "boundaries": "Mechanisms are often derived from mouse models or inferred from human correlative data. The review acknowledges complexity and multiple interacting pathways rather than a single linear mechanism.",
